WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.470
If I showed you two big intersections — one
with a bunch of signs, traffic lights, and

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:11.059
boundaries, and one with no markings or apparent
rules at all — which one would look safer

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.860
to you?

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:18.619
Towns all over Europe are starting to experiment
with streets like this: where cars, bikes,

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:22.500
buses, and people can travel freely in the
same space.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.320
I think this looks like an accident waiting
to happen.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.130
But I know someone who would know a lot more
about this than I do.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.270
I’m Roman Mars from 99 Percent Invisible.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:35.739
And that’s what urban planners often refer
to as a “shared space” design strategy.

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:40.320
It seems counterintuitive, but there’s evidence
that getting rid of all signs and barriers

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.329
might make our streets a lot safer.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:45.149
So — how does it work?

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:50.040
There’s a spot in Google street view where
you can witness a town undergoing this exact

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.560
transformation.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.160
This is Poynton, England — about 20 minutes
away from Manchester.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.820
Here’s the city center in May 2011: there’s
a mess of signs and lights, a few small sidewalks,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:04.080
and some haphazard guardrails to keep pedestrians
safe.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.200
And here it is in July 2015.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.600
Traffic lights, road signs, curbs — all
gone.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.860
“Something very strange is happening on
the streets of Britain — I don’t know

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:19.480
if you’ve noticed, but in some places, pavements
and roads have been blurring into one with

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.280
cars, buses, and pedestrians all sharing the
same space.”

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.760
The town spent 4 million pounds to expand
sidewalk space and strip the city center of

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:28.720
traditional demarcations.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.680
Now, the only marker left is this little sign:
Poynton shared space village.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.600
The concept is that the absence of separation
will make everyone more cautious — so commuters

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.780
slow down, make eye contact, and negotiate.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.240
Watch what happens when a boy in Poynton is
encouraged to cross the street without waiting

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.620
for the cars to clear the intersection.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.340
Because cars don’t spend time waiting at
traffic lights, it takes less time on average

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.280
for them to get through the crossing.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:10.200
Even when bikers and pedestrians are absent,
cars drive slow due to a concept called “edge

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.200
friction.”

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.080
It’s the idea that nearby vertical elements
in a driver’s peripheral vision — like

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.640
trees or lamps, create a visual cue for how
fast they’re going.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.330
On a highway, those are often totally absent,
so the sidelines blur.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:29.390
In a shared space, those lines play an important
mental trick to slow down drivers.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.980
In theory, shared space works well for pedestrians,
allowing them to follow their desired path

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.280
while walking.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.110
Instead of being limited to a strict path,
they can walk exactly in the direction they

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.110
want to go.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.920
n
In practice, that doesn’t always seem to

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.290
be the case.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:47.440
Video footage of a shared space at Elwick
Square in Ashford, England shows that pedestrians’

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.660
mostly still stick to crosswalks, or where a crosswalk would be.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.040
That’s largely because these layouts are
stressful: The majority of a survey group

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.050
asked about crossing Elwick Square reported
feeling anxious about it.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:05.640
And those pedestrians frequently report that
they prefer things the way they used to be.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.420
But the “way things used to be” is relative.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:13.500
If you look at old footage of city streets
in the early 1900s, cars mixed freely with

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.300
pedestrians, bicyclists, and streetcars.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:20.660
Vehicles couldn’t go very fast back then,
so there wasn’t a huge concern about separating

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.690
them from walkers and bikers.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:27.150
With plenty of city streets, that’s still
the case without any intentional urban planning.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.640
But the big question is whether these deliberately
stripped-down designs actually make people

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.319
safer.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.959
It seems that in many cases, they do.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.940
For one thing, we know that the number of
accidents drops after shared spaces are installed.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:47.290
In Ipswich, rates of accidents involving injuries
fell from 23 over three years to just one

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.500
per year.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.540
In London’s Kensington High Street, the
number of pedestrians injured dropped by nearly

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:53.540
60 percent.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:59.069
And in Drachten in the Netherlands, accidents
at one intersection fell from 36 in four years

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:01.060
to two in two years.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:06.100
We also know that shared spaces are quantifiably
more free-flowing based on analysis of traffic

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:06.740
conflicts.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:11.420
With video footage like this from Exhibition
Road in London, analysts can rate the severity

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.600
of a traffic conflict based on participants’
speed and change of course.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.840
Before the shared space renovation, one pedestrian
had to step back onto the sidewalk to avoid

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.280
a departing car.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.530
Another broke out into a sprint to avoid getting
hit by an oncoming van.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.620
After the shared space installation, traffic
conflicts were less frequent and less severe on

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.490
average — like this, where pedestrians wait
for a cab to cross before continuing.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:38.280
Or this one, where a cyclist changes course
to avoid pedestrians.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.460
But that doesn’t mean these designs work
for everyone.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.650
“I think it’s the most ludicrous idea
I’ve ever heard.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.240
What about blind people?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.320
Children?”

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.600
From the beginning, shared space designs have
been under fire for providing insufficient

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.800
protections for disabled pedestrians, especially
the visually impaired.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.320
“The family of a pensioner who died a month
after being hit by a bus have called for traffic

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.690
lights to be reinstalled at the junction.”

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:14.170
“Traffic lights here were recently removed to make
way for a so-called shared space, but it’s

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.180
a move that has angered David’s family”

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:19.600
“I’m sure if the traffic lights were there,

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.260
this wouldn’t have happened”

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:27.880
A 2015 House of Lords report called for a
temporary ban on shared space designs.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.770
And in August 2016 a select committee of the
House of Commons launched an inquiry into

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:35.650
the accessibility of such environments.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:40.139
Their final report in April 2017 asked the
government to put all shared space schemes

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:44.660
on hold until they improved the process of
consulting disabled communities.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:50.420
Preliminary designs like these by the Danish
Building Research Institute give us an idea

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.270
of what that balance might look like.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:56.990
They include both the mixed traffic of shared
space and the raised street textures and button-activated

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:00.280
crosswalks that disabled users are used to.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.961
Shared spaces can be effective, but for this
kind of plan to work across communities, it

00:06:04.961 --> 00:06:10.050
will require a lot more research to determine
what will work in different places and what will serve

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.009
the needs of all the people sharing the streets.

